        "location": "ledger.py:53; constructor at ledger.py:11",
        "requirement": "Preserve valid dictionary inputs and existing batch behavior. Successful-key replays must not mutate balances again.",
        "evidence": "Run with python3 -B: `from collections import Counter; from ledger import Ledger; x = Ledger(Counter({'a': 10, 'b': 0})); x.batch([]); print(dict(x.balances))`. Observed {'a': 20, 'b': 0}; expected {'a': 10, 'b': 0}. Counter is a valid dict subclass, and balances.copy() preserves it. Batch commit calls Counter.update(), which adds values instead of replacing them. A fresh one-unit transfer batch produces {'a': 19, 'b': 1}; replay produces {'a': 38, 'b': 2}. Baseline probes retain {'a': 10, 'b': 0} for the empty batch and {'a': 9, 'b': 1} after both transfer and replay. These are manifestations of one root cause.",
        "suggested_fix": "Normalize constructor input into an owned built-in dict, while retaining public dictionary identity thereafter. Add Counter regression coverage for empty batches, transfers and replay."
      }
    ],
